    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 231135\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n435 AM PDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Above normal temperatures continue through next week.\n\n* Isolated storm chances linger into early next week, with best \n  chances in northwestern Arizona and the southern Great Basin.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Through Saturday.\n\nWith a fairly persistent pattern expected, temperatures remain\nabove normal through the forecast period. Ensemble guidance \nkeeps the 500mb ridge anchored in southern NM and southeastern AZ,\nleaving us under south-southwest flow aloft. A subtle cooldown is\nexpected Monday-Tuesday as moisture increases in our southeastern\nareas, but excessive heat quickly returns by mid-week and sticks \naround. Elevated low temperatures will offer little-to-no \novernight relief, especially in the Mojave Desert. Major to \nextreme heatrisk is forecast to return to these areas during the \nsecond half of the week.\n\nThe aforementioned south-southwest flow is just southerly enough\nto let moisture linger but southwesterly enough to keep it \nlimited. These conditions should favor isolated afternoon \nconvection, primarily in northwestern AZ and the southern Great \nBasin where odds are 10-30%. Main concerns with this activity are \ngusty outflow winds and dry lightning, though brief heavy \ndownpours are possible. An influx of moisture on Monday may result\nin an uptick in coverage along/southeast of I-15, but models \nstill disagree on the northwestward extent and magnitude of this \ninflux. During the second half of the week, the flow acquires a \ngreater westerly component, allowing moisture and precipitation \nchances to subside.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Winds\nwill become variable early this morning, with a brief \nsoutheasterly shift mid- to late morning as winds increase to \n8-10KT. Through early afternoon, winds veer to the southwest, \nincreasing to 10-15KT with gusts to 20-25KT expected through \nsunset. Gusts diminish thereafter, with winds remaining southerly \nto southwesterly through the end of the period. VFR conditions \nwill continue with no operationally significant cloud cover \nexpected. Temperatures will exceed 100 degrees from 18Z-03Z, \nreaching a high of 109 degrees this afternoon.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 12Z Forecast Package...Somewhat variable\nwinds around 5-10KT this morning will veer to the south-southwest\nby early afternoon and become gusty, with gusts t0 20-25KT \ncommon. Gusts will diminish after sunset, with winds becoming \nlighter and settling out of typical patterns overnight. An \nexception to this will be the western Mojave Desert/KDAG, where \nwinds will become light and variable late morning, with gusty \nsouthwesterly winds to 25-30KT returning late afternoon into \ntonight. Additionally, the northern Owens Valley/KBIH will follow \ntypical daily patterns, with gusty up-valley winds developing this\nafternoon before veering to the north through the evening. \nIsolated thunderstorms are expected this afternoon, mainly over \nthe higher terrain of the southern Great Basin and northwestern \nArizona, with gusty outflow winds and lightning strikes the main \nconcerns. Confidence in direct impacts at area terminals is low \nat this time. VFR conditions prevail with FEW-SCT clouds at or \nabove 10kft.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods \nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
